`GET /api/pricing` returns the full machine-readable list.

Long-context tier: some models reprice the whole request once the prompt reaches 200K tokens, mirroring the provider's official pricing. The Grok family (grok-4.5, grok-4.3, grok-build-0.1) charges 2x above 200K (e.g. grok-4.5 is $5.00 / $18.00 per 1M). Google's Gemini Pro models (gemini-2.5-pro, gemini-3.1-pro) charge 2x input / 1.5x output above 200K (e.g. gemini-2.5-pro is $2.50 / $15.00 per 1M). OpenAI's GPT-6 and GPT-5.x families (gpt-6-astra, gpt-6-sol, gpt-6-luna, gpt-5.6-*, gpt-5.5, gpt-5.5-pro, gpt-5.4, gpt-5.4-pro) charge 2x input / 1.5x output above 272K (e.g. gpt-5.5 is $10.00 / $45.00 per 1M). `GET /api/pricing` reflects the base tier.

Cached input: `GET /api/pricing` publishes each model's cache rates beside its input and output rates — `cacheReadPricePerMillion`, and `cacheWritePricePerMillion` where the vendor charges a separate cache write (Anthropic, OpenAI GPT-5.6 and later) — plus `longContextCacheReadPricePerMillion` / `longContextCacheWritePricePerMillion` on the long-context side. `GET /v1/models` carries the same numbers as `pricing.cache_read` / `pricing.cache_write`. A model with no cache keys bills cached tokens at its input rate. These are absolute per-1M rates, not multipliers, and they follow the vendor's own sheet per model (grok-4.5 and grok-4.6 share an input rate and cache at different rates), so derive the ratio rather than assuming one. They apply to post-paid metering (user.blockrun.ai and api.blockrun.ai bill exact upstream usage from this sheet); the x402 rail's per-call quote is a pre-call token estimate.

