## Decide — typed judgments (free, different host)

**Not on this gateway.** Served by `api.blockrun.ai/v1/decide` with a registered
API key from https://user.blockrun.ai — no payment header and no wallet path. It
is free because a judgment costs less to serve than the smallest amount x402 can
settle, so metering it would cost more than the answer.

Send a state and up to 64 questions in one call; get typed answers back.

| Type | You define | You get back |
|------|-----------|--------------|
| `noul` | nothing — true or false | how strongly the state supports the claim |
| `choice` | a map of label to meaning, 2-255 | the top label plus a share for every option |
| `score` | the rungs, in words, 2-255 | a weighted position across them, plus the distribution |

```
POST https://api.blockrun.ai/v1/decide
authorization: Bearer <your key>
{"state": "...", "questions": {"urgent": {"type": "noul", "instructions": "Is this urgent?"}}}
```

**What the number is.** All three types sit on natural-language inference: the
state is the premise, the question the hypothesis. For a `choice` the per-option
scores are divided by their total, which destroys absolute strength — three
options the model supports equally weakly come back looking identical to two it
supports equally strongly. It is a share of the agreement found, not the
probability the answer is correct. Pick thresholds from your own labelled
examples, not by reading the number as a percentage.

**First call after an idle period takes about 32 seconds.** The container is
cold and has to start; warm calls return in under one and a half seconds, and a
call carrying four questions is no slower than one carrying a single question.
Set your client timeout above 60s for the first call or you will time out and
conclude the endpoint is down. It is not per request — it happens once after a
quiet stretch.

**The number is not a probability that the answer is correct**, and the
confidence does not tell you when it is wrong. On 2026-09-21 this endpoint read
"Oh fantastic, another outage. Really loving this service today." as positive at
0.9378, and read a literal complaint as negative at 0.9507 — a right answer and
a wrong one at effectively the same confidence, so no threshold separates them.
Gate on the gap between the top two options instead, which catches "none of
these labels fit"; nothing in the response catches confident sarcasm. Worked
examples: https://blockrun.ai/text-classification-api

**OpenJev is not Jev.** The backend is an unaffiliated open-source NLI
cross-encoder (MIT) that we host. It is not TypeSafe's Jev, we do not resell
Jev, and we have not benchmarked the two — so we quote no gap. The
`x-blockrun-backend` header names what served every call.

Full reference: https://blockrun.ai/docs/api-reference/decide

